{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310685,"nonce":"y2qBVYdf1B0E/mq8QE9O8dYSJiA=","title":"FIXED ada97141: the duplicate-release guard matched only IrInstruction::Free, so call-shaped releases were invisible and the repeatedly-run ...","message":"**FOUND AND FIXED 2026-08-19 (`ada97141`) — by BUILDING THE FREE-GRAPH VIEWER\n(`021ceec6`) AND READING IT, after days of failed bisection.**\n\n**THE DEFECT.** A release takes FOUR shapes:\n\n| kind | release |\n|---|---|\n| plain | `IrInstruction::Free` |\n| string | `CallDirect haxe_string_free` |\n| array | `CallDirect haxe_array_free` then `Free` on the header |\n| anon | `CallDirect rayzor_anon_drop` |\n\nBoth duplicate guards in `insert_free.rs` matched **only** `IrInstruction::Free`\n(`matches!(i, IrInstruction::Free { ptr } if *ptr == alloc_id)`). The other three\nwere invisible. **The duplicate arises WITHIN ONE RUN, not across runs** — I got this wrong in\nthe original commit message and corrected it in `e4c551e4`. Cross-run dedup\nalready works: `has_free` (insert_free.rs ~504) matches `CallDirect` against\n`dealloc_ids`, and `dealloc_ids` includes `string_free_ids` (~486), so a value\nreleased in run N is dropped from `allocs_needing_free` in run N+1. Measured: the\npass runs 6 times on `Main.main` and its release count goes 11 → 20 → 20 → 20 →\n20 → 20, max 1 per allocation.\n\nThe real mechanism: THREE emitters place a string release — on a path leaving\nthe function (~659), at the end of the defining block (~747), and at a loop latch\n(~818) — and `allocs_needing_free` is computed once, in Step 2, BEFORE any\nrelease is inserted, and is never pruned as they are added. A value matching two\nrules is released twice in a single run.\n\n**WHY A DUPLICATE STRING RELEASE IS FATAL.** `runtime/src/haxe_string.rs`\n`haxe_string_free` poisons the header (`ptr=null, len=0, cap=0`) so a second call\ncannot free the byte buffer twice — its comment claims the duplicate \"becomes a\nno-op\". **It does not**: the last line is `drop(Box::from_raw(s))`, run\nUNCONDITIONALLY. So the second call frees the header block a second time, and\nreads that block after it was freed to check the poison. Classic deferred\ncorruption: the freelist is only walked at some LATER allocation, which is why\nthe crash lands nowhere near the cause.\n\n**FIX:** one helper, `already_released(function, value, string_free_id,\narray_free_id, anon_drop_id)`, recognising all four shapes.\n\n**THE 96b992fb A/B I RAN WAS INVALID — OWN THIS.** I concluded \"my fix is\nexonerated\" from an A/B whose two arms gave identical results. They did, because\nBOTH arms double-free the 24-byte header: before `96b992fb` the latch emitted a\nbare libc `Free` on the string header, after it a second `haxe_string_free` —\nbehind the same blind guard either way. **An A/B in which both arms are broken\nproves nothing.** The duplicate-creating rule actually landed at `ac5eae18`\n(2026-08-13), which bounds the regression window.\n\n**RESULT:** `nue/bench/eval` — dead partway for the whole session — now scores\nall 6 chunks, exit 0, 3/3 runs, positions=1530. 184/184 haxe tests, clippy clean,\nllama-chat unchanged (78.8 tok/s, no traps).\n\n**STILL OPEN:** 4 duplicate releases remain in `BPETokenizer.encodeBPE`, all at\n`LastUse` sites — that insertion path has NO guard at all, not merely a blind\none. They did not stop the harness completing, but they are the same double-free\nshape and should be closed. The graph lists them.\n\n**THE LESSON THAT MATTERS.** Bisecting by source edits and exit codes failed for\ndays because the symptom is deferred and layout-dependent. What worked was\nmaking the pass's DECISIONS visible and sorting them by what looked wrong. When\na bug's symptom is far from its cause, instrument the decision, not the crash.\n\n\n**WHY THE FAILURE LOOKED NONDETERMINISTIC — SETTLED, AND IT IS NOT A RACE.**\nThe corrupting write is deterministic; only the DETECTION POINT moves. The\nsecond `haxe_string_free` writes 24 zero bytes into an already-freed block; in a\nsmall-size zone those bytes are freelist links. What occupies that block by then\ndecides the symptom:\n- still on the free list, untouched → immediate abort in `_xzm_free` (133)\n- handed out and rewritten → garbage ptr/cap is read and deallocated\n  (134 POINTER_BEING_FREED_WAS_NOT_ALLOCATED, or 139)\n- links overwritten, damage latent → caught at an arbitrary LATER malloc\n  (`_xzm_xzone_malloc_freelist_outlined`, 133)\nThat is why the named frame is a different innocent bystander every run and why\nsurvival ranged over 0/1/2/4 chunks from ONE binary. Invariant across runs: the\nmachine code (byte-identical artifacts) and the arithmetic (`ppl=22.8316` every\ntime a chunk completed) — no run ever emitted garbage, which itself argues for a\ndouble free rather than a premature free of live data.\n\n**RACE RULED OUT FIVE WAYS:** `NUE_MATMUL_WORKERS=1` crashed 6/6; `NUE_MATMUL=0`\n(no SpinPool built at all) crashed; a 3-thread process with both non-main threads\nparked in `semaphore_wait_trap` reproduced the whole family; the faulting thread\nwas main in 37/37 archived crash reports; and 10 CPU burners stretched runtime\n8s → 60–100s with 7/7 unchanged outcomes.\n\n**EXIT 132 / SIGILL IS A DIFFERENT BUG** (the trap-stub path) and must not be\nattributed to this one.\n\n**WHY `--max-chunks 1` ALWAYS PASSED:** the `break` precedes `pos += n`, so a\nsingle chunk never reaches the latch — the second emitter never fires.\n\n\n**REMAINING DUPLICATES — RE-AUDITED 2026-08-19, AND MY OWN COUNT WAS WRONG.**\nAfter the fix, 4 allocations still show two `StringFree` calls, all in\n`BPETokenizer.encodeBPE`. Re-checking them properly:\n\n**Two releases are NOT a double free when they sit on paths that cannot both run\nfor the SAME allocation.** My first audit tested plain CFG reachability, which\ntraverses the loop BACK EDGE — so two arms of a diamond inside a loop body\n(b90 → b95 ← b92) looked \"mutually reachable\" when in fact they run on different\nITERATIONS, releasing a different allocation each time. Recounting with back\nedges removed (same-iteration reachability) drops one: **3 candidates, not 4**,\nand `%2419` is fine.\n\n**The right test for a duplicate is: can both releases run for one allocation —\ni.e. does one reach the other WITHOUT passing the definition again.** Plain\nreachability over-counts; \"released anywhere in the function\" over-suppresses.\n\n**AN ATTEMPTED EXTENSION WAS REVERTED, DELIBERATELY.** I guarded the other two\nemitters (path-leaving ~659, end-of-defining-block ~747) with the same helper\nplus an `at` block. Measured effect on the eval workload: **NONE** — allocations\n483, double-frees 4, unreleased 236, byte-identical before and after. And the\nguard uses back-edge-traversing reachability, so inside a loop it can suppress a\nrelease on an exclusive arm and LEAK. Risk with no measured benefit ⇒ reverted.\nDo not re-add it without a workload that shows it firing, and use\nsame-iteration reachability when you do.\n\n**HEAD (`e4c551e4`) verified after revert:** eval 3/3 exit 0, 6 chunks;\n184/184 haxe tests.\n\n\n**RUNTIME DETECTOR — VALIDATED BOTH WAYS (`8cc2ecb0`).** `RZT_DBG_STRFREE=1` now\nKEEPS released headers instead of reclaiming them, so the mark written into a\nreleased header survives and a second release is named:\n\n    [strfree] DOUBLE FREE of 0xcabee10a0 — already released\n\n| build | reports | eval |\n|---|---|---|\n| before the guard fix (`ada97141~1`) | **5** | exit 0 (the detector suppresses the second free) |\n| HEAD | **0** | exit 0, 6 chunks |\n\n**THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS DETECTOR DID NOT WORK AND I NEARLY BELIEVED IT.** It\nwrote the mark but still reclaimed the header, so the mark could not be read\nback — it reported 0 on a build with a KNOWN double free. \"Zero reports\" from an\nunvalidated probe means nothing. Always run the probe against a build where the\nbug is present before trusting a clean result.\n\n**THE 3 REMAINING encodeBPE CANDIDATES ARE NOT REAL.** The validated detector\nreports 0 across a full 6-chunk run at HEAD, so those static hits are\nover-counting by the audit, not live double frees. A static pair of releases is\nonly a double free if both can run FOR THE SAME allocation.\n\n**`scripts/build.sh`** does the two-step (lower the library with the current\ncompiler, then rebuild the CLI so it embeds it) and FAILS if the result does not\ncarry a matching library. Use it after any `compiler/src` change — skipping it\ndoes not error, it silently swaps in the lower-from-source path, which behaves\ndifferently. That mistake cost time twice in one session.\n\n\n**THE DOUBLE-FREE CLASS IS CLOSED BY CONSTRUCTION, not merely patched.** The\npass has three placement rules, and only ONE pair can ever collide:\n`entry_allocs` and `inner_allocs` are split by an if/else on the defining block\n(insert_free.rs ~627), so they are disjoint, and `rotation_handled` values are\nexcluded from both. The latch rule alone draws from the whole\n`allocs_needing_free`, so latch x (entry|inner) is the only overlap — which is\nexactly what `ada97141` guards. Guarding the other two emitters was tried and\nmeasured: **zero effect**, because they cannot collide. Reverted.\n\n**THE 236 \"NEEDS RELEASE, NONE INSERTED\" ARE NOT A HOT-PATH LEAK — MEASURED.**\nAll 236 are defined outside the entry block, so they fall to the inner rule,\nwhich only fires when the value is confined to its defining block. Splitting\nthem by whether their defining block can reach a normal Return:\n- **105 cannot return at all** — they are the concat chains inside `throw`\n  messages (`throw \"KVCache overflow: \" + ... + \" \u003e \" + maxSeqLen`). They cost\n  nothing unless the throw fires.\n- **131 are on live paths**, but dominated by ONE-TIME work: `Main.main` 30,\n  `GGUFLoader.loadWithTokenizer` 22, `LlamaArch.buildLinear` 17,\n  `buildBlock` 10 — plus diagnostics (`FlashDecode.decodeBatch`'s 13 are the\n  `[flash-batch]` println; `dumpArgmax`, `checkShape` are debug helpers).\n`KVCache.append` and `Linear.forward` look alarming in the raw count but are\nentirely throw-path. **There is no unbounded per-token leak here** — do not\nspend a dataflow rewrite on this number without measuring first.\n\n**STATIC PLACEMENT ANALYSIS OVER-REPORTS; THE RUNTIME CHECK IS EXACT.** A static\ngate over the graph flagged 3 double frees at HEAD while the validated runtime\ndetector reported 0 — two releases on branches that never run for the same\nallocation are correct, and the CFG alone cannot always tell (a back-edge set\nfrom natural-loop analysis is incomplete for irreducible flow). So the shipped\ngate is `scripts/check_double_free.sh`, which runs a program under\n`RZT_DBG_STRFREE` and asserts no report. Validated both ways: 200 reports on a\n200-iteration concat loop against `ada97141~1`, none at HEAD. The static graph\nstays a DIAGNOSTIC (the viewer), not a gate.\n\n- Index summary moved from MEMORY.md (2026-10-18): FIXED ada97141: dup-release guard saw only `IrInstruction::Free`. TOOL: `RAYZOR_DUMP_FREE_GRAPH`.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_release_guard_blind_to_call_shaped_frees` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}